-- title:
--   Section of a smooth relative curve is an effective Cartier divisor
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ and $T$ be schemes, and let $p \colon X \to T$ be a morphism which is separated and smooth of relative dimension $1$. Let $\sigma \colon T \to X$ be a section of $p$, that is, $\sigma$ followed by $p$ equals $\mathrm{id}_T$. Then the ideal sheaf data $\sigma.\mathtt{ker}$ attached to $\sigma$ — the kernel of the map $\mathcal{O}_X \to \sigma_*\mathcal{O}_T$ induced by $\sigma$ — satisfies the predicate `IsInvertible`, which here means, unfolded: for every point $x \in X$ there exist an affine open $U \subseteq X$ and a section $f \in \Gamma(X, U)$ with $x$ lying in the basic open $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\, f$, together with an element $g$ of $\Gamma(X, D(f))$ that is a non-zerodivisor in that ring, where $D(f)$ denotes the basic open of $f$ regarded as an affine open of $X$, such that the ideal cut out by $\sigma.\mathtt{ker}$ on $D(f)$ is the principal ideal generated by $g$. No hypotheses are imposed on the base $T$: it need not be reduced, Noetherian or locally of finite type.
--
--   This is the statement that the image of a section of a separated smooth relative curve is a relative effective Cartier divisor, the local principal generator being a non-zerodivisor; classically it is the codimension-one case of the fact that a section of a smooth morphism is a regular immersion. It underlies the divisor-theoretic constructions on curve models in the project, and is cited by the development of divisor classes, of the sheaves of sections associated with such divisors, and of ramification indices along points.
